If you are running Workbench 1.3 or later, be sure to include MOUNT=1 in 
the Mountlist to force the new floppy driver to start immediately.  In
this case the drive will detect a diskette immediately when you insert
it, and the icon for the diskette will appear on the Workbench screen.

However, if you are using Workbench 1.2, DO NOT include MOUNT=1 in the
Mountlist.  The Mount command provided with Workbench 1.2 does not recog-
nize the MOUNT=1 parameter in the Mountlist.  In this case the new driver
will not become active until you make a direct access to the drive by
name from CLI.  This means that when you insert a diskette for the first
time after Mounting the drive, the drive will not "notice" the diskette,
and an icon for the diskette will NOT appear on the Workbench screen. 
Once you have "activated" the drive via direct access from CLI, it will
continue to fiunction normally, and icons for diskettes will appear on
the Workbench screen as normal.

If you are using Workbench 1.3.2 you may see a spurious error requester
immediately after Mounting the new driver.  The message warns:

	NO DISK PRESENT IN UNIT x

This message appears only if there is no disk in the Mac drive.  Simply
click on Cancel to clear the requester.  We do not yet know why this
message appears, but it is harmless.


3.		New Version of DiskCopy

If you DUPLICATE a diskette from Workbench or drag the icon for one
diskette over another, Workbench invokes the Diskcopy program to perform
the copy.  You can also run Diskcopy from CLI to duplicate diskettes. 
However, the original Diskcopy program does NOT work properly with the
Mac-2-Dos disk drive.  We have now fixed this problem by modifying the
Diskcopy program.  The modified version of Diskcopy is on the Mac-2-Dos
diskette.

To use the modified Diskcopy program, you must copy it from the Mac-2-Dos
disk into the SYSTEM drawer of your Workbench disk.  To do this, just
drag the DiskCopy icon from the Mac-2-Dos window into the SYSTEM drawer. 
Alternately, rom CLI just use the COPY command to copy DiskCopy into the
SYS:SYSTEM directory.

NOTE: The modified DiskCopy program reads many tracks from the source
disk and then writes them to the destination disk, instead of performing
the copy one track at a time.  Also, the modified DiskCopy ALWAYS
verifies the copy, although it still accepts (and ignores) the NOVERIFY
keyword.

UPDATE NOTE: If you received this Mac-2-Dos version as an update, you
MUST install the new macfloppy.device driver in the DEVS: directory for
the modified DiskCopy to work properly.  To install the new version of
the driver, simply COPY the driver to the DEVS: directory.  Be sure that
the name of the driver is all lower-case.  If you accidentally type the
name in upper-case, the MOUNT step will fail and the Mac-2-Dos drive will
NOT function as an AmigaDOS device.


4.		AMAX COMPATIBILITY

Mac-2-Dos Package B includes a Macintosh disk drive which you can use
as an Amiga floppy disk drive.  The Mac disk drive provided with
Mac-2-Dos Package B is a standard, unmodified Macintosh external floppy
disk drive, which will work with ANY Macintosh and with Mac-2-Dos.  But
AMAX does NOT work with all Mac drives, and unfortunately this is one
of the Mac disk drives AMAX does not support.  There is nothing that we
can do about this situation, because it is an AMAX problem, not a
Mac-2-Dos problem.  The makers of AMAX are aware of the problem. 

Mac-2-Dos (Package A or B) DOES work when connected to the Amiga
through AMAX.  Mac-2-Dos and AMAX can co-exist on an Amiga; this means
that they do not interfere with each other, and either can function
separately and independently of the other.  But neither AMAX nor
Mac-2-Dos software can work with the hardware of the other package.


